6405203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405751} :step/id #uuid "33729004-b50e-5d2f-834e-d49725efa1e1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405229}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405229 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405744} :step/id #uuid "3c4b870b-8c96-5748-a850-278f9c3ea827" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405246 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405193} :step/id #uuid "412874e7-71c8-5502-a19e-44a4c5765cf5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405247 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405783} :step/id #uuid "4129c234-e9d8-59b9-858e-593f406380ba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405168}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405088} :step/id #uuid "500a960b-d094-5e4d-837f-39eddffd5599"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405803} :step/id #uuid "9b33b557-7783-5bc2-917b-0a8f37b681f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405137} :step/id #uuid "aa1cb56f-f5cf-54cb-acd9-960b646cf6c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405479} :step/id #uuid "b545f6fb-318f-5799-8417-3e43953888d6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405247}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405245} :step/id #uuid "d844ea27-8b17-5fbb-ad56-d4f9d28782f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405832} :step/id #uuid "f08aca7c-b062-580e-b874-dc9d45aa927a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405246}}] :activity/title "MTH 255 — OHW-2" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045461} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405598}] [[:tutorial/id #uuid "4f8c08b2-5695-5a91-88a9-a89a3a427871"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405300 :tutorial/content "Source: [MTH255-WHW-Week1_v1.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-1%2FMTH255-WHW-Week1_v1.pdf)\nDue: 2026-10-01\n\n## Instructions\n\nShow clear, organized work or a written explanation for every problem where required. Some problems are graded for correctness; an answer without supporting work does not earn full credit.\n\nYou may write on a printed copy, annotate the PDF digitally, or use your own paper with the same problem layout. Submit one seven-page PDF to **Gradescope → Written Homework 1**: the cover page followed by one page per problem, including blank problems. If a solution uses several sheets, combine them into one page for that problem. The source warns that submissions in the wrong format receive zero credit. See the linked PDF for the complete instructions.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." :tutorial/id #uuid "4f8c08b2-5695-5a91-88a9-a89a3a427871" :tutorial/title "Assignment information"}] [[:question/id #uuid "a17bf913-7f21-5bd1-ab10-c2d76180be2b"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405551 :question/id #uuid "a17bf913-7f21-5bd1-ab10-c2d76180be2b" :question/problem "**Question 1 — Part A**\n\nShow that its height is $\\lVert\\mathbf v\\rVert\\sin\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "Drop a perpendicular from the tip of $\\mathbf v$ to the line along $\\mathbf u$. In the resulting right triangle, $\\sin\\theta=h/\\lVert\\mathbf v\\rVert$ for the acute angle shown. Thus $h=\\lVert\\mathbf v\\rVert\\sin\\theta$. For an obtuse included angle, the right triangle uses $\\pi-\\theta$ and gives the same result.\n\nThe height is the component of $\\mathbf v$ perpendicular to the base, not its full length. Identify the hypotenuse and opposite side in your triangle; $\\sin(\\pi-\\theta)=\\sin\\theta$ covers an obtuse included angle."}] [[:question/id #uuid "72764034-8319-5088-8a08-690c2046e292"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405411 :question/id #uuid "72764034-8319-5088-8a08-690c2046e292" :question/problem "**Question 1 — Part B**\n\nShow that the area is $\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert$." :question/worked-solution "Area is base times perpendicular height: $A=\\lVert\\mathbf u\\rVert\\,\\lVert\\mathbf v\\rVert\\sin\\theta$. By the magnitude formula for a cross product, this equals $\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert$.\n\nUse $\\lVert\\mathbf u\\rVert$ as the base length and the height from Part A. The cross product is a vector; its magnitude is the nonnegative scalar area. Parallel vectors give zero area."}] [[:question/id #uuid "9a2e296b-a844-5cc3-9353-12489716d182"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405530 :question/id #uuid "9a2e296b-a844-5cc3-9353-12489716d182" :question/problem "**Question 1 — Part C**\n\nLet $\\mathbf u=\\langle u_1,u_2,u_3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf v=\\langle v_1,v_2,v_3\\rangle$. Show that the area is\n\n$$A=\\left\\lVert\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\u_1&u_2&u_3\\\\v_1&v_2&v_3\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rVert.$$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding along the first row gives the vector $\\langle u_2v_3-u_3v_2,\\ u_3v_1-u_1v_3,\\ u_1v_2-u_2v_1\\rangle=\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v$. Its magnitude is the area by Part B. Equivalently, $A=\\sqrt{(u_2v_3-u_3v_2)^2+(u_3v_1-u_1v_3)^2+(u_1v_2-u_2v_1)^2}$.\n\nThe determinant with basis vectors in its first row is cross-product notation, so its expansion is a vector. Keep the minus sign in the middle cofactor and take the vector magnitude to obtain area."}] [[:step/id #uuid "40278735-a000-5feb-b258-2e16563a7bb9"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405551} :step/id #uuid "40278735-a000-5feb-b258-2e16563a7bb9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405221}}] [[:step/id #uuid "39e0b2ed-74d8-5fd1-8603-095cb3a48069"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405411} :step/id #uuid "39e0b2ed-74d8-5fd1-8603-095cb3a48069" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405205}}] [[:step/id #uuid "345fb758-79b1-504f-bd3a-331aa23e4bd3"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405530} :step/id #uuid "345fb758-79b1-504f-bd3a-331aa23e4bd3"}] [[:multistep/id #uuid "99c3ae3a-448c-526b-96b7-0c561f1a8187"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405527 :multistep/context "Source question: 1.\n\n**Area of a parallelogram**\n\nConsider the parallelogram formed by $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf v$. Let $\\theta$ be the angle between them.\n\n![Parallelogram formed by u and v with included angle theta](images/ca/ca9c54ad7c41bea1e72278f7d24742fbde8a4f6a4abf46b7ddadf557dad7bae4.png)" :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405243} :multistep/id #uuid "99c3ae3a-448c-526b-96b7-0c561f1a8187" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405530} :step/id #uuid "345fb758-79b1-504f-bd3a-331aa23e4bd3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405411} :step/id #uuid "39e0b2ed-74d8-5fd1-8603-095cb3a48069" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405551} :step/id #uuid "40278735-a000-5feb-b258-2e16563a7bb9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405221}}]}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "b0c40836-f4ac-508d-bc37-dd42df713496"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405527} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "b0c40836-f4ac-508d-bc37-dd42df713496" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050155}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405589}] [[:question/id #uuid "b6678591-0a57-529e-afed-2a52cbbc4d11"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405614 :question/id #uuid "b6678591-0a57-529e-afed-2a52cbbc4d11" :question/problem "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\nShow that the height of the box shown is $\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert\\cos\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "The vector $\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v$ is normal to the base. With unit normal $\\mathbf n=(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)/\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert$, the projection of $\\mathbf w$ on that normal is $\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert\\cos\\theta$. For the acute angle shown, this projection is the height. For arbitrary orientation, geometric height is $\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert|\\cos\\theta|$.\n\nHere the angle is measured from the normal, so use cosine. The worksheet formula assumes the acute angle drawn; an obtuse angle gives a negative signed projection, whose absolute value is the physical height. Assume a nondegenerate base when defining its unit normal."}] [[:question/id #uuid "3021b788-f4db-57a9-8db3-90426b903e88"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405194 :question/id #uuid "3021b788-f4db-57a9-8db3-90426b903e88" :question/problem "**Question 2 — Part B**\n\nShow that the volume is $|\\mathbf w\\cdot(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)|$. The quantity $\\mathbf w\\cdot(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)$ is the scalar triple product of $\\mathbf u$, $\\mathbf v$, and $\\mathbf w$." :question/worked-solution "The base area is $\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert$ and the height is $\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert|\\cos\\theta|$. Their product is $|\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert\\cos\\theta|=|\\mathbf w\\cdot(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)|$.\n\nThe dot product measures the component of $\\mathbf w$ along the base normal, multiplied by the base area. Its sign records orientation; absolute value gives nonnegative volume. A degenerate base or a side lying in the base plane gives zero volume."}] [[:question/id #uuid "9ebd1eb7-31ef-51b3-b552-fe69f2171cfa"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405544 :question/id #uuid "9ebd1eb7-31ef-51b3-b552-fe69f2171cfa" :question/problem "**Question 2 — Part C**\n\nLet $\\mathbf u=\\langle u_1,u_2,u_3\\rangle$, $\\mathbf v=\\langle v_1,v_2,v_3\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf w=\\langle w_1,w_2,w_3\\rangle$. Show that the volume is\n\n$$V=\\left|\\det\\begin{pmatrix}w_1&w_2&w_3\\\\u_1&u_2&u_3\\\\v_1&v_2&v_3\\end{pmatrix}\\right|.$$" :question/worked-solution "The scalar triple product expands to $w_1(u_2v_3-u_3v_2)+w_2(u_3v_1-u_1v_3)+w_3(u_1v_2-u_2v_1)$. This is the first-row cofactor expansion of the determinant with rows $\\mathbf w,\\mathbf u,\\mathbf v$. Taking its absolute value gives the volume from Part B.\n\nUnlike the determinant notation in Problem 1, all entries here are scalars, so the determinant is a scalar. Check the alternating cofactor signs and retain the outer absolute value."}] [[:step/id #uuid "6325a316-192f-54f2-8602-53bc8c6281f5"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405614} :step/id #uuid "6325a316-192f-54f2-8602-53bc8c6281f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405800}}] [[:step/id #uuid "f16f2b01-b52d-5f68-9717-f4c02611825d"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405194} :step/id #uuid "f16f2b01-b52d-5f68-9717-f4c02611825d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405764}}] [[:step/id #uuid "e78bbd3e-686a-51a0-9360-51f589e73e6b"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405764 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405544} :step/id #uuid "e78bbd3e-686a-51a0-9360-51f589e73e6b"}] [[:multistep/id #uuid "14fd2662-45c2-5d65-b77a-013277de5979"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405108 :multistep/context "Source question: 2.\n\n**Volume of a parallelepiped**\n\nConsider the slanted box formed by $\\mathbf u$, $\\mathbf v$, and $\\mathbf w$. Let $\\theta$ be the angle between $\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v$ and $\\mathbf w$.\n\n![Parallelepiped with base vectors u and v, side w, and normal u cross v](images/f6/f647f7a54f2626f5257f1ed859525799f16e7f825c34e50d200bc4f21151dd3f.png)" :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405349} :multistep/id #uuid "14fd2662-45c2-5d65-b77a-013277de5979" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405614} :step/id #uuid "6325a316-192f-54f2-8602-53bc8c6281f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405764 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405544} :step/id #uuid "e78bbd3e-686a-51a0-9360-51f589e73e6b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405194} :step/id #uuid "f16f2b01-b52d-5f68-9717-f4c02611825d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405764}}]}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "200af8bb-8ee9-5c88-ab54-112581b73bbf"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405108} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "200af8bb-8ee9-5c88-ab54-112581b73bbf" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050362}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405135}] [[:question/id #uuid "2de99047-ea27-55a6-81ee-f6ea1f3cd5f8"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405187 :question/id #uuid "2de99047-ea27-55a6-81ee-f6ea1f3cd5f8" :question/problem "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nDifferentiate $c=f(x,y)$ with respect to $x$, using the chain rule on the right. Conclude that $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{f_x}{f_y}$." :question/worked-solution "Since $c$ is constant and $y=y(x)$, the chain rule gives $0=f_x\\,dx/dx+f_y\\,dy/dx=f_x+f_y\\,dy/dx$. If $f_y\\ne0$, solving gives $dy/dx=-f_x/f_y$.\n\nBoth paths in the dependence tree contribute. The direct $x$ path gives $f_x$ because $dx/dx=1$; the $y$ path gives $f_y\\,dy/dx$. Division by $f_y$ requires $f_y\\ne0$."}] [[:answer/id #uuid "816d6b52-f829-5c07-9f81-9667bf9d020a"] {:answer/id #uuid "816d6b52-f829-5c07-9f81-9667bf9d020a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-x/y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406082}] [[:answer-field/id #uuid "f569cfb2-021a-5c26-848b-a4c125593d68"] {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "816d6b52-f829-5c07-9f81-9667bf9d020a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-x/y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406082} :answer-field/id #uuid "f569cfb2-021a-5c26-848b-a4c125593d68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406305}] [[:question/id #uuid "8a4c59a9-98c0-5117-98f1-aa1ac38cd7f0"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "816d6b52-f829-5c07-9f81-9667bf9d020a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-x/y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406082} :answer-field/id #uuid "f569cfb2-021a-5c26-848b-a4c125593d68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406305}] :question/id #uuid "8a4c59a9-98c0-5117-98f1-aa1ac38cd7f0" :question/problem "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nUse Part A to find $dy/dx$ for the circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "With $f=x^2+y^2$, $f_x=2x$ and $f_y=2y$. Thus $dy/dx=-2x/(2y)=-x/y$ where $y\\ne0$. Direct differentiation gives the same equation, $2x+2y\\,dy/dx=0$. At $(\\pm1,0)$ the tangent is vertical."}] [[:question/id #uuid "dd117d7e-f3a2-5134-9e0e-3ab8f8e2b9af"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405731 :question/id #uuid "dd117d7e-f3a2-5134-9e0e-3ab8f8e2b9af" :question/problem "**Question 3 — Part C**\n\nDraw $x^2+y^2=1$ and a couple of tangent lines. Compare their slopes with the derivative from Part B." :question/worked-solution "Draw the unit circle and, for example, tangents at $(0,1)$ and $(1/\\sqrt2,1/\\sqrt2)$. The first tangent is $y=1$ with slope $0=-0/1$. The second is $y=-x+\\sqrt2$ with slope $-1=-(1/\\sqrt2)/(1/\\sqrt2)$. Both agree with $dy/dx=-x/y$. Other correctly drawn and checked tangent points are valid.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/73/730c9f459fe4f68e944d2fa043f43b145dd3b0b237ebb20533b13aefd6bdacad.png)\n\nLabel each chosen point and tangent. A tangent to a circle is perpendicular to its radius, which provides a geometric check on the slope. If you choose $(\\pm1,0)$, use a vertical tangent and explain why $-x/y$ is undefined there."}] [[:step/id #uuid "c115f30c-1944-5d54-9b55-d23f508d9139"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405187} :step/id #uuid "c115f30c-1944-5d54-9b55-d23f508d9139" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405801}}] [[:step/id #uuid "f18de478-6a29-5826-9a70-d241aefb98a9"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405484} :step/id #uuid "f18de478-6a29-5826-9a70-d241aefb98a9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405586}}] [[:step/id #uuid "afdf84c3-31ae-5e42-94a2-ed8da2cc7685"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405731} :step/id #uuid "afdf84c3-31ae-5e42-94a2-ed8da2cc7685"}] [[:multistep/id #uuid "f035b023-8e26-5b16-b106-3d3ee958a249"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405788 :multistep/context "Source question: 3.\n\n**Implicit differentiation — single variable**\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be a scalar function with level curve $f(x,y)=c$. Regard $y$ as a function of $x$, so the dependence tree is:\n\n![f depends on x and y, and both branches depend on x](images/c9/c90c3e7d7c66ca010b15db00d492aa6278a31550025ce0353a2647a7412c4fa6.png)" :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405649} :multistep/id #uuid "f035b023-8e26-5b16-b106-3d3ee958a249" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405731} :step/id #uuid "afdf84c3-31ae-5e42-94a2-ed8da2cc7685"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405187} :step/id #uuid "c115f30c-1944-5d54-9b55-d23f508d9139" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405801}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405484} :step/id #uuid "f18de478-6a29-5826-9a70-d241aefb98a9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405586}}]}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "3f41e426-021b-5e0f-850b-85a8da8fe58c"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405788} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "3f41e426-021b-5e0f-850b-85a8da8fe58c" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051090}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405240}] [[:question/id #uuid "3e831647-6617-58b1-8d0e-7be3292e7716"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405238 :question/id #uuid "3e831647-6617-58b1-8d0e-7be3292e7716" :question/problem "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nConclude that $\\langle\\varphi_y,-\\varphi_x\\rangle$ is tangent to the level curve at $(x,y)$." :question/worked-solution "Where $\\varphi_y\\ne0$, a tangent direction is $\\langle1,dy/dx\\rangle=\\langle1,-\\varphi_x/\\varphi_y\\rangle$. Multiplying by $\\varphi_y$ gives $\\langle\\varphi_y,-\\varphi_x\\rangle$, so it is tangent. If $\\varphi_y=0$ but $\\varphi_x\\ne0$, solving locally for $x(y)$ instead gives a vertical tangent, also represented by this vector.\n\nMultiplying a nonzero tangent vector by a nonzero scalar preserves its tangent line, even if the direction reverses. A meaningful tangent direction requires a regular point: $\\nabla\\varphi\\ne\\mathbf0$. At a critical point this formula may give the zero vector and does not determine a tangent."}] [[:answer/id #uuid "1a61c7af-9ede-5a61-9a77-314eed0a607b"] {:answer/id #uuid "1a61c7af-9ede-5a61-9a77-314eed0a607b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405883}] [[:answer-field/id #uuid "db19c9b3-1894-5904-ae21-ee74228ce68f"] {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a61c7af-9ede-5a61-9a77-314eed0a607b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405883} :answer-field/id #uuid "db19c9b3-1894-5904-ae21-ee74228ce68f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406254}] [[:question/id #uuid "0d72a085-32f0-50ef-84b8-517e4dcb493e"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a61c7af-9ede-5a61-9a77-314eed0a607b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405883} :answer-field/id #uuid "db19c9b3-1894-5904-ae21-ee74228ce68f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406254}] :question/id #uuid "0d72a085-32f0-50ef-84b8-517e4dcb493e" :question/problem "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nCompute the dot product, then explain why $\\nabla\\varphi$ is perpendicular to the level curve at $(x,y)$.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle\\varphi_y,-\\varphi_x\\rangle=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\langle\\varphi_x,\\varphi_y\\rangle$, so the dot product is $\\varphi_x\\varphi_y-\\varphi_y\\varphi_x=0$. At a regular point, the nonzero vector from Part A spans the tangent line; therefore the nonzero gradient is normal to the curve. Include this geometric conclusion with the calculation."}] [[:step/id #uuid "6e7d4b58-fcd1-5a46-b281-11a251d0a17f"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405238} :step/id #uuid "6e7d4b58-fcd1-5a46-b281-11a251d0a17f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405688}}] [[:step/id #uuid "cf68eb88-e289-5d7b-b25d-70ab43d638f7"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405087} :step/id #uuid "cf68eb88-e289-5d7b-b25d-70ab43d638f7"}] [[:multistep/id #uuid "7b8d7f31-40e1-5d67-9f68-fdca314f6dbe"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405438 :multistep/context "Source question: 4.\n\n**The 2D gradient is orthogonal to a level curve**\n\nLet $\\varphi(x,y)$ be a scalar function and consider $\\varphi(x,y)=c$. From Problem 3, $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{\\varphi_x}{\\varphi_y}$." :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405390} :multistep/id #uuid "7b8d7f31-40e1-5d67-9f68-fdca314f6dbe" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405238} :step/id #uuid "6e7d4b58-fcd1-5a46-b281-11a251d0a17f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405087} :step/id #uuid "cf68eb88-e289-5d7b-b25d-70ab43d638f7"}]}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "4fc001b4-4f6d-5efc-82e8-917c8b65fa94"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405438} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "4fc001b4-4f6d-5efc-82e8-917c8b65fa94" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405301}] [[:question/id #uuid "62269627-33f0-548a-bd98-964ed5e629d0"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405346 :question/id #uuid "62269627-33f0-548a-bd98-964ed5e629d0" :question/problem "**Question 5 — Part A**\n\nDifferentiate $c=f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $x$, using the chain rule on the right. Conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=-\\dfrac{f_x}{f_z}$ and, similarly, $\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=-\\dfrac{f_y}{f_z}$." :question/worked-solution "Holding $y$ fixed gives $0=f_x(1)+f_y(0)+f_z z_x=f_x+f_z z_x$. Thus $z_x=-f_x/f_z$ if $f_z\\ne0$. Holding $x$ fixed similarly gives $0=f_x(0)+f_y(1)+f_z z_y$, hence $z_y=-f_y/f_z$.\n\nWhen differentiating with respect to $x$, the independent coordinate $y$ stays fixed but the dependent coordinate $z$ changes. Keep the $f_z z_x$ chain-rule term. The formulas require $f_z\\ne0$, the condition for solving locally for $z(x,y)$."}] [[:answer/id #uuid "3ce766d6-2bf4-59a0-83d3-05575cf599dc"] {:answer/id #uuid "3ce766d6-2bf4-59a0-83d3-05575cf599dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-x/z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405947}] [[:answer-field/id #uuid "9430ad15-ca36-5c27-8658-8e993f714a5e"] {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce766d6-2bf4-59a0-83d3-05575cf599dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-x/z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405947} :answer-field/id #uuid "9430ad15-ca36-5c27-8658-8e993f714a5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406114}] [[:question/id #uuid "2b1ffd25-40f0-5a3a-81ff-3d56a039ac3f"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce766d6-2bf4-59a0-83d3-05575cf599dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-x/z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405947} :answer-field/id #uuid "9430ad15-ca36-5c27-8658-8e993f714a5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406114}] :question/id #uuid "2b1ffd25-40f0-5a3a-81ff-3d56a039ac3f" :question/problem "**Question 5 — Part B**\n\nUse Part A to find $\\partial z/\\partial x$ for the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$.\n\n$\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For $f=x^2+y^2+z^2$, the partial derivatives are $f_x=2x$ and $f_z=2z$. Holding $y$ fixed gives $z_x=-2x/(2z)=-x/z$ for $z\\ne0$. The term $y^2$ has zero partial derivative with respect to $x$."}] [[:question/id #uuid "0b35d9ec-b384-595e-85ab-21d85a676a98"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405084 :question/id #uuid "0b35d9ec-b384-595e-85ab-21d85a676a98" :question/problem "**Question 5 — Part C**\n\nDraw the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$. Fix a value of $y$ and draw a couple of tangent lines to the resulting circle. Compare their slopes with Part B." :question/worked-solution "One valid choice is the slice $y=0$, giving $x^2+z^2=1$ in the $xz$-plane. At $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1)$, draw the tangent $z=1$, with slope $dz/dx=0$. At $(1/\\sqrt2,0,1/\\sqrt2)$, draw $z=-x+\\sqrt2$ within the same plane, with slope $-1$. Both agree with $z_x=-x/z$. Show the slice on the sphere; other nondegenerate slices and correctly checked tangent points are valid.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/47/47d98e418cee25adf036004c66cffb1de67b2898235229ebd28921b92ea5f2cb.png)\n\nFor a slice $y=y_0$, the circle is $x^2+z^2=1-y_0^2$, so choose $|y_0|<1$. Measure slope in the $xz$-plane while keeping $y$ fixed. At $z=0$, the slice tangent is vertical and $-x/z$ is undefined."}] [[:step/id #uuid "11c80a48-2afe-5975-b84c-517f776a592d"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405346} :step/id #uuid "11c80a48-2afe-5975-b84c-517f776a592d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405328}}] [[:step/id #uuid "5b7936e7-c49a-5870-b96c-740872fa0933"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405176} :step/id #uuid "5b7936e7-c49a-5870-b96c-740872fa0933" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405334}}] [[:step/id #uuid "5dd3c3ed-0489-56be-a773-8eb8a944c187"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405084} :step/id #uuid "5dd3c3ed-0489-56be-a773-8eb8a944c187"}] [[:multistep/id #uuid "862cc95f-2d16-59de-83e5-3c4048895e63"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405470 :multistep/context "Source question: 5.\n\n**Implicit differentiation — multivariable**\n\nLet $f(x,y,z)$ be a scalar function and consider the level surface $f(x,y,z)=c$. Regard $z$ as a function of independent variables $x$ and $y$, so $\\partial y/\\partial x=\\partial x/\\partial y=0$. The dependence tree is:\n\n![f depends on x, y, and z; each branch is differentiated with respect to x and y](images/e6/e62ca88ac96b7eff736b8f6e72079a49562861ebcec69b3be4150e5efe087c90.png)" :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405099} :multistep/id #uuid "862cc95f-2d16-59de-83e5-3c4048895e63" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405346} :step/id #uuid "11c80a48-2afe-5975-b84c-517f776a592d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405328}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405176} :step/id #uuid "5b7936e7-c49a-5870-b96c-740872fa0933" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405084} :step/id #uuid "5dd3c3ed-0489-56be-a773-8eb8a944c187"}]}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "7402b070-a8d3-54db-b8fd-5e5160f9774c"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405470} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "7402b070-a8d3-54db-b8fd-5e5160f9774c" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050164}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405416}] [[:question/id #uuid "8f3b824d-a891-57ed-abdc-50d8dec94ac5"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405501 :question/id #uuid "8f3b824d-a891-57ed-abdc-50d8dec94ac5" :question/problem "**Question 6 — Part A**\n\nShow that $\\langle\\varphi_z,0,-\\varphi_x\\rangle$ and $\\langle0,\\varphi_z,-\\varphi_y\\rangle$ are tangent to the level surface at $(x,y,z)$." :question/worked-solution "Assume $\\varphi_z\\ne0$ and parametrize the surface locally by $\\mathbf r(x,y)=\\langle x,y,z(x,y)\\rangle$. Its coordinate tangent vectors are $\\mathbf r_x=\\langle1,0,-\\varphi_x/\\varphi_z\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r_y=\\langle0,1,-\\varphi_y/\\varphi_z\\rangle$. Multiplying each by $\\varphi_z$ gives the two stated tangent vectors.\n\nThe first tangent follows a curve with $y$ fixed, and the second follows one with $x$ fixed. When $\\varphi_z\\ne0$, they are independent and span the tangent plane. If that partial derivative vanishes at a regular point, choose another coordinate with nonzero partial derivative for the local parametrization."}] [[:answer/id #uuid "6992794b-2451-5ed4-990b-b2b7e9ac34c1"] {:answer/id #uuid "6992794b-2451-5ed4-990b-b2b7e9ac34c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406040}] [[:answer-field/id #uuid "9dea8db5-9891-5066-b480-712a6ab830b3"] {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6992794b-2451-5ed4-990b-b2b7e9ac34c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406040} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dea8db5-9891-5066-b480-712a6ab830b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406138}] [[:answer/id #uuid "bfa8d899-b6c7-5a8a-b35f-ffed5db34f14"] {:answer/id #uuid "bfa8d899-b6c7-5a8a-b35f-ffed5db34f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406202}] [[:answer-field/id #uuid "1ccab6a9-af78-5252-a0ed-7638554b0fef"] {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa8d899-b6c7-5a8a-b35f-ffed5db34f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406202} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ccab6a9-af78-5252-a0ed-7638554b0fef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405886}] [[:question/id #uuid "378c93ed-8654-5c14-84c7-05d98e1e60b2"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa8d899-b6c7-5a8a-b35f-ffed5db34f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406202} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ccab6a9-af78-5252-a0ed-7638554b0fef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6992794b-2451-5ed4-990b-b2b7e9ac34c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406040} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dea8db5-9891-5066-b480-712a6ab830b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406138}] :question/id #uuid "378c93ed-8654-5c14-84c7-05d98e1e60b2" :question/problem "**Question 6 — Part B**\n\nCompute both dot products. Conclude that $\\nabla\\varphi$ is perpendicular to both vectors and hence to the level surface at $(x,y,z)$.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle\\varphi_z,0,-\\varphi_x\\rangle=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle0,\\varphi_z,-\\varphi_y\\rangle=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For $\\nabla\\varphi=\\langle\\varphi_x,\\varphi_y,\\varphi_z\\rangle$, the products are $\\varphi_x\\varphi_z-\\varphi_z\\varphi_x=0$ and $\\varphi_y\\varphi_z-\\varphi_z\\varphi_y=0$. When $\\varphi_z\\ne0$, Part A supplies two independent tangent directions, so orthogonality to both makes the gradient normal to the tangent plane. Include this conclusion, not just the two zeros."}] [[:answer/id #uuid "16c1e865-e3ed-537d-97d1-c47f55ec216f"] {:answer/id #uuid "16c1e865-e3ed-537d-97d1-c47f55ec216f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(phi_x*phi_z,phi_y*phi_z,phi_z^2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405876}] [[:answer-field/id #uuid "13047fbc-a783-5536-8f4a-3a29b9bb361f"] {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c1e865-e3ed-537d-97d1-c47f55ec216f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(phi_x*phi_z,phi_y*phi_z,phi_z^2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405876} :answer-field/id #uuid "13047fbc-a783-5536-8f4a-3a29b9bb361f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405869}] [[:question/id #uuid "54030489-7b70-5752-923e-1d1ccfff7638"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c1e865-e3ed-537d-97d1-c47f55ec216f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(phi_x*phi_z,phi_y*phi_z,phi_z^2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405876} :answer-field/id #uuid "13047fbc-a783-5536-8f4a-3a29b9bb361f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405869}] :question/id #uuid "54030489-7b70-5752-923e-1d1ccfff7638" :question/problem "**Question 6 — Part C**\n\nAlternatively, compute the cross product below. What does the result tell you about $\\nabla\\varphi$ and the tangent plane?\n\n$\\langle\\varphi_z,0,-\\varphi_x\\rangle\\times\\langle0,\\varphi_z,-\\varphi_y\\rangle=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cross product is $\\langle\\varphi_x\\varphi_z,\\varphi_y\\varphi_z,\\varphi_z^2\\rangle=\\varphi_z\\nabla\\varphi$. A cross product of two independent tangent vectors is normal to their plane. For $\\varphi_z\\ne0$, it is a nonzero scalar multiple of the gradient, so the gradient is also normal. If $\\varphi_z=0$, these particular vectors do not establish the normal; a different local coordinate choice is needed at a regular point."}] [[:step/id #uuid "c5d522b7-2ff8-565a-a2d5-971b51d214a7"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405501} :step/id #uuid "c5d522b7-2ff8-565a-a2d5-971b51d214a7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405831}}] [[:step/id #uuid "fc723400-8f4d-5c38-9d2f-415e7016e56d"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405831 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405213} :step/id #uuid "fc723400-8f4d-5c38-9d2f-415e7016e56d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405512}}] [[:step/id #uuid "930ae6f2-4bf3-519b-ae21-b3c9e2fd0053"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405311} :step/id #uuid "930ae6f2-4bf3-519b-ae21-b3c9e2fd0053"}] [[:multistep/id #uuid "a275c6a2-50ca-57bf-9cae-18b542d4439e"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405556 :multistep/context "Source question: 6.\n\n**The 3D gradient is orthogonal to a level surface**\n\nLet $\\varphi(x,y,z)$ be a scalar function with level surface $\\varphi(x,y,z)=c$. From Problem 5, $\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=-\\dfrac{\\varphi_x}{\\varphi_z}$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=-\\dfrac{\\varphi_y}{\\varphi_z}$." :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405656} :multistep/id #uuid "a275c6a2-50ca-57bf-9cae-18b542d4439e" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405311} :step/id #uuid "930ae6f2-4bf3-519b-ae21-b3c9e2fd0053"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405656 :ste