6-8ae5-ecc6d14dd1cf" :question/problem "Textbook problem **1.2.8.a**.\n\n**Question 1**\n\nLet $a$ be a nonzero real number and let $c$ be a real number. Verify that\n\n$$\ny=(x-c)^a\n$$\n\nis a solution of\n\n$$\ny'=ay^{(a-1)/a}\n$$\n\non $(c,\\infty)$." :question/worked-solution "For $x>c$, the base $x-c$ is positive, so all displayed real powers are defined and the exponent laws apply. Differentiating gives $y\\prime=a(x-c)^{a-1}$. Meanwhile, $ay^{(a-1)/a}=a[(x-c)^a]^{(a-1)/a}=a(x-c)^{a-1}$. The two sides agree for every $x\\in(c,\\infty)$, verifying the solution.\n\nUse the chain rule, including the derivative $1$ of $x-c$. The condition $a\ne0$ makes the exponent $(a-1)/a$ meaningful; $x>c$ is essential when $a$ is an arbitrary real number."}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "47bd3318-94e3-558f-8129-3f9f98974de4"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405078} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "47bd3318-94e3-558f-8129-3f9f98974de4" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051090}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405272}] [[:answer/id #uuid "f0690942-2bab-5278-affe-f8a5497e1655"] {:answer/id #uuid "f0690942-2bab-5278-affe-f8a5497e1655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "r/lambda+(G_0-r/lambda)*e^(-lambda*t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406292}] [[:answer-field/id #uuid "a11d4ba7-9dbf-5069-8b03-90be1042854d"] {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0690942-2bab-5278-affe-f8a5497e1655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "r/lambda+(G_0-r/lambda)*e^(-lambda*t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406292} :answer-field/id #uuid "a11d4ba7-9dbf-5069-8b03-90be1042854d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406143}] [[:answer/id #uuid "569d9afd-9652-5622-a4d3-1e83387b121b"] {:answer/id #uuid "569d9afd-9652-5622-a4d3-1e83387b121b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "r/lambda" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406007}] [[:answer-field/id #uuid "e9dbaa9b-8186-5089-a5a7-36731b8c0103"] {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "569d9afd-9652-5622-a4d3-1e83387b121b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "r/lambda" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406007} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9dbaa9b-8186-5089-a5a7-36731b8c0103" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406281}] [[:question/id #uuid "36f72ed9-a0cf-588e-bde2-1df93ea2f549"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0690942-2bab-5278-affe-f8a5497e1655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "r/lambda+(G_0-r/lambda)*e^(-lambda*t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406292} :answer-field/id #uuid "a11d4ba7-9dbf-5069-8b03-90be1042854d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406143} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "569d9afd-9652-5622-a4d3-1e83387b121b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "r/lambda" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406007} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9dbaa9b-8186-5089-a5a7-36731b8c0103" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406281}] :question/id #uuid "36f72ed9-a0cf-588e-bde2-1df93ea2f549" :question/problem "Textbook problem **2.1.43**.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nExperiments indicate that glucose is absorbed by the body at a rate proportional to the amount of glucose present in the bloodstream. Let $\\lambda$ denote the (positive) constant of proportionality. Now suppose glucose is injected into a patient's bloodstream at a constant rate of $r$ units per unit of time. Let $G=G(t)$ be the number of units in the patient's bloodstream at time $t>0$. Then\n\n$$\nG'=-\\lambda G+r,\n$$\n\nwhere the first term on the right is due to the absorption of the glucose by the patient's body and the second term is due to the injection. Determine $G$ for $t>0$, given that $G(0)=G_0$. Also, find\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}G(t).\n$$\n\n$G(t)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}G(t)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The integrating factor is $e^{\\lambda t}$, giving $(e^{\\lambda t}G)\\prime=re^{\\lambda t}$. Hence $G=r/\\lambda+Ce^{-\\lambda t}$, with $C=G_0-r/\\lambda$. Because $\\lambda>0$, the exponential tends to zero. Differentiation verifies $G\\prime=-\\lambda G+r$, and $G(0)=G_0$. This is a mathematical model, with equilibrium $r/\\lambda$."}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "2cfdfcb5-06f8-515e-b5dd-7a6cf3e36d10"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405211} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "2cfdfcb5-06f8-515e-b5dd-7a6cf3e36d10" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049780}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405182}] [[:question/id #uuid "b93c29c5-b86b-5ae9-8499-cc25a76c055c"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405624 :question/id #uuid "b93c29c5-b86b-5ae9-8499-cc25a76c055c" :question/problem "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nAn integral curve that is a solution curve." :question/worked-solution "Draw and label the upper hyperbola branch $y=\\sqrt{x^2+1/4}$. Draw the visible arc for $|x|<\\sqrt3/2$, within the supplied plotting window. The function is a solution for every real $x$: $y\\prime=x/\\sqrt{x^2+1/4}=x/y$. It stays above the singular line $y=0$.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/7f/7f0bf50470f063b2555f5ac202e8bb1ef934ac29d9fc7bf4726d2d79de612da6.png)\n\nThe curve must be tangent to the field at each point and must be a single-valued differentiable graph on an interval. A branch with $y\ne0$ avoids the undefined right-hand side."}] [[:question/id #uuid "69e45544-bf18-5e99-9dc5-8b465e7cc190"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405373 :question/id #uuid "69e45544-bf18-5e99-9dc5-8b465e7cc190" :question/problem "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nAn integral curve that is not a solution." :question/worked-solution "Using the worksheet’s geometric meaning of integral curve, draw the entire right branch $x^2-y^2=1/4$, $x>0$, including its turn at $(1/2,0)$. One parametrization is $(x,y)=(\\tfrac12\\cosh s,\\tfrac12\\sinh s)$. Draw its visible portion within the supplied axes. Away from $y=0$, its slope is $dy/dx=\\cosh s/\\sinh s=x/y$. The whole curve is not a solution graph $y(x)$: for $x>1/2$ it has two $y$ values, and the explicit ODE is undefined at its vertical tangent $(1/2,0)$.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/1a/1a426ad2bc023da1fc079d9ca7017c8735d1495114389a0d020cf1874195071d.png)\n\nHere “integral curve” means a geometric trajectory following the line directions, including a vertical turn; it is broader than a classical solution graph of $y\\prime=x/y$. The parametric tangent $(\\tfrac12\\sinh s,\\tfrac12\\cosh s)=(y,x)$ remains nonzero at the turn. Each upper/lower portion can separately define a solution for $x>1/2$, but their union cannot."}] [[:question/id #uuid "28e9d899-99ee-5600-9524-0629a7b3b393"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405171 :question/id #uuid "28e9d899-99ee-5600-9524-0629a7b3b393" :question/problem "**Question 3 — Part C**\n\nA curve that is not an integral curve." :question/worked-solution "Draw the horizontal line $y=1/2$ and label it “not an integral curve.” Its slope is $0$, whereas the field has slope $2x$ there. For example, at $(1/2,1/2)$ the field slope is $1$, so the line crosses the field directions rather than following them.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/9d/9d76186dcece593fb5232a41983bd98bbf55dde24b49d6af5430171ceb90c249.png)\n\nAn isolated point of tangency is insufficient. An integral curve must follow the field throughout its regular portions. Check the tangent mismatch at a specific point."}] [[:step/id #uuid "4f0dbf9f-588f-5d07-8e0a-dee2f95b7682"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405624} :step/id #uuid "4f0dbf9f-588f-5d07-8e0a-dee2f95b7682" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405474}}] [[:step/id #uuid "8715e248-ad20-5616-9aa6-02efb1da94bb"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405373} :step/id #uuid "8715e248-ad20-5616-9aa6-02efb1da94bb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405727}}] [[:step/id #uuid "dc060909-fbde-54ab-bdf4-e877866a84a9"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405727 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405171} :step/id #uuid "dc060909-fbde-54ab-bdf4-e877866a84a9"}] [[:multistep/id #uuid "ac83bc65-e101-5c37-ab8f-09ba2fd53da9"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405578 :multistep/context "Source question: 3.\n\nThe direction field for\n\n$$\ny'=\\frac{x}{y}\n$$\n\nis provided below. On the direction field sketch and label the following:\n\n![Direction field for y prime equals x over y](images/2a/2ab36389693808aef75de9ab5f665a9956c672a1a6f12c850a380991b08abbd8.png)" :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405297} :multistep/id #uuid "ac83bc65-e101-5c37-ab8f-09ba2fd53da9" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405624} :step/id #uuid "4f0dbf9f-588f-5d07-8e0a-dee2f95b7682" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405474}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405373} :step/id #uuid "8715e248-ad20-5616-9aa6-02efb1da94bb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405727}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405727 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405171} :step/id #uuid "dc060909-fbde-54ab-bdf4-e877866a84a9"}]}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "df010457-65d2-5af9-b384-977b8ae9816a"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405578} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "df010457-65d2-5af9-b384-977b8ae9816a" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405735}] [[:question/id #uuid "fa3e5d36-38b5-5c37-97cd-541aae5d3cdb"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405822 :question/id #uuid "fa3e5d36-38b5-5c37-97cd-541aae5d3cdb" :question/problem "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nAn integral curve that is a solution curve." :question/worked-solution "In the upper-left part of the supplied field, trace the upper arc of a small closed trajectory surrounding approximately $(-2,0)$. Use only the portion above the $x$-axis between its left and right vertical turns, excluding those endpoints. Label this arc “solution curve.” It follows the local directions and gives one $y$ for each $x$ on that open interval.\n\nUse the displayed field to trace the arc; no exact equation is supplied. A solution graph must be single-valued and have finite derivative on its interval, so stop before the vertical turns rather than including a complete loop."}] [[:question/id #uuid "0e6f5ea5-7fa9-549e-b8f3-ca2305a6870d"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405090 :question/id #uuid "0e6f5ea5-7fa9-549e-b8f3-ca2305a6870d" :question/problem "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nAn integral curve that is not a solution." :question/worked-solution "Trace a complete small closed trajectory around the center near $(-2,0)$, following the field continuously through the upper arc, right turn, lower arc, and left turn. Label the whole loop “integral curve, not a solution graph.” The loop has two heights for many $x$ values and vertical turns, so the entire loop cannot be represented by a differentiable single-valued $y(x)$.\n\nThe worksheet uses “integral curve” geometrically for a traced trajectory. Match the field’s tangent directions rather than drawing an arbitrary circle; its exact shape need not be circular. The distinction from part (a) is the whole closed trajectory versus a graph segment."}] [[:question/id #uuid "a3ad5074-7608-5a0b-b227-e249a8477dc7"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405560 :question/id #uuid "a3ad5074-7608-5a0b-b227-e249a8477dc7" :question/problem "**Question 4 — Part C**\n\nA curve that is not an integral curve." :question/worked-solution "Draw a short horizontal segment near $(0,0)$ and label it “not an integral curve.” The supplied field is nearly vertical in that neighborhood, so the segment visibly cuts across the direction marks.\n\nThe task only needs one counterexample. Choose a region where the mismatch between the segment’s tangent and the field is clear; no inferred differential equation is necessary."}] [[:step/id #uuid "2ddba153-cfaf-587d-b7f6-672f57461a6c"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405186 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405822} :step/id #uuid "2ddba153-cfaf-587d-b7f6-672f57461a6c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405757}}] [[:step/id #uuid "e34002aa-9e7d-5b65-a7a7-d20b02cb6aeb"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405090} :step/id #uuid "e34002aa-9e7d-5b65-a7a7-d20b02cb6aeb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405047}}] [[:step/id #uuid "003748fd-17d4-5b51-bba2-5c11a7d52337"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405560} :step/id #uuid "003748fd-17d4-5b51-bba2-5c11a7d52337"}] [[:multistep/id #uuid "5f910c0c-f925-5be8-83e1-32b240647491"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405340 :multistep/context "Source question: 4.\n\nOn the provided direction field sketch and label the following:\n\n![Direction field provided for Problem 4](images/05/05f804c32b466c20fed261a3932b5748cde3835c787f3a96bfb9aa9e4b9456fc.png)" :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405186} :multistep/id #uuid "5f910c0c-f925-5be8-83e1-32b240647491" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405560} :step/id #uuid "003748fd-17d4-5b51-bba2-5c11a7d52337"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405186 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405822} :step/id #uuid "2ddba153-cfaf-587d-b7f6-672f57461a6c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405090} :step/id #uuid "e34002aa-9e7d-5b65-a7a7-d20b02cb6aeb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405047}}]}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "78bcf688-168c-5155-b59e-537387414c48"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405340} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "78bcf688-168c-5155-b59e-537387414c48" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405425}] [[:step/id #uuid "829de6c7-587d-59b1-9551-2b26fcd06aa4"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405256} :step/id #uuid "829de6c7-587d-59b1-9551-2b26fcd06aa4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405268}}] [[:step/id #uuid "46151900-dd0e-5341-a33c-31f08e2e81fb"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405272} :step/id #uuid "46151900-dd0e-5341-a33c-31f08e2e81fb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405343}}] [[:step/id #uuid "60e34426-45e0-525b-9681-9a7023e94329"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405343 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405182} :step/id #uuid "60e34426-45e0-525b-9681-9a7023e94329" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405289}}] [[:step/id #uuid "4d0d5766-90e9-59f4-b56d-1d3c73f3ed6d"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405735} :step/id #uuid "4d0d5766-90e9-59f4-b56d-1d3c73f3ed6d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405491}}] [[:step/id #uuid "8c1eb0a5-02d2-5a63-a245-a5ba289649f7"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405425} :step/id #uuid "8c1eb0a5-02d2-5a63-a245-a5ba289649f7"}] [[:activity/id #uuid "105799b2-3037-57ca-951e-b5a3f18df304"] {:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405044} :activity/due #inst "2026-10-03T06:59:59.000Z" :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405457} :activity/id #uuid "105799b2-3037-57ca-951e-b5a3f18df304" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405272} :step/id #uuid "46151900-dd0e-5341-a33c-31f08e2e81fb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405343}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405735} :step/id #uuid "4d0d5766-90e9-59f4-b56d-1d3c73f3ed6d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405491}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405343 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405182} :step/id #uuid "60e34426-45e0-525b-9681-9a7023e94329" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405256} :step/id #uuid "829de6c7-587d-59b1-9551-2b26fcd06aa4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405268}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405425} :step/id #uuid "8c1eb0a5-02d2-5a63-a245-a5ba289649f7"}] :activity/title "MTH 256 — WHW-1" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045461} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405097}] [[:tutorial/id #uuid "3f266a7a-9f91-53eb-b355-b6c67b3a9be2"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405239 :tutorial/content "Source: [MTH341-WHW1.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FWHW-1%2FMTH341-WHW1.pdf)\nDue: 2026-10-07\n\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), by Lyryx Learning, based on the original text by K. Kuttler. Exercise text reproduced from the CC BY textbook; see its license page.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphs are authored additions to the linked textbook exercises.\n\n## Instructions\n\nPut your solutions on separate sheets of paper (8.5 × 11 inches or A4). Use the original assignment sheet as a cover sheet and fill in your name and student ID. Submit your homework on Canvas. The assignment sheet states that late homework is not accepted." :tutorial/id #uuid "3f266a7a-9f91-53eb-b355-b6c67b3a9be2" :tutorial/title "Assignment information"}] [[:question/id #uuid "311d12c0-a56a-5aea-bb69-3187ddb6e2d3"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405197 :question/id #uuid "311d12c0-a56a-5aea-bb69-3187ddb6e2d3" :question/problem "Source exercise: **1.1.1**, [printed page 7 (PDF page 19)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=19).\n\n**Question 1**\n\nGraphically, find the point $(x_1,y_1)$ which lies on both lines,\n\n$$\nx+3y=1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4x-y=3.\n$$\n\nThat is, graph each line and see where they intersect." :question/worked-solution "Plot $y=(1-x)/3$ using, for example, $(0,1/3)$ and $(1,0)$. Plot $y=4x-3$ using $(0,-3)$ and $(3/4,0)$. Draw both lines on the same axes and mark their common point.\n\nThe graph places the intersection near $(0.77,0.08)$. Its exact coordinates are $\\left(\\frac{10}{13},\\frac{1}{13}\\right)$.\n\n![Reference graph showing both lines and their intersection](images/6b/6b7c1afd7bdc97e66c0477241907e1be5bc065b6e7f456182fc68b509d2012a5.png)\n\nA solution must lie on both lines, so look for their crossing rather than either line's intercept. Your response should include two labeled lines on common axes and an intersection consistent with your plotting scale. The exact point checks both equations: $10/13+3(1/13)=1$ and $4(10/13)-1/13=3$. An algebraic answer alone does not complete the requested graph."}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "91b0e312-e352-505f-b5cd-b2d66830b9f6"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405197} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "91b0e312-e352-505f-b5cd-b2d66830b9f6" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050796}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405510}] [[:question/id #uuid "210d7e79-79c0-5def-9165-30e59785183b"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405141 :question/id #uuid "210d7e79-79c0-5def-9165-30e59785183b" :question/problem "Source exercise: **1.1.2**, [printed page 7 (PDF page 19)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=19).\n\n**Question 2**\n\nGraphically, find the point of intersection of the two lines\n\n$$\n3x+y=3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x+2y=1.\n$$\n\nThat is, graph each line and see where they intersect." :question/worked-solution "Rewrite the lines as $y=3-3x$ and $y=(1-x)/2$. The first passes through $(0,3)$ and $(1,0)$; the second passes through $(0,1/2)$ and $(1,0)$. Plot and label both lines on the same axes. Their common point is $(1,0)$.\n\n![Reference graph showing the two lines intersecting at one point on the x-axis](images/21/212eb96c55d7bb1d6a9700e6334558773624ae385aeeb7698c16ff86b7c2f058.png)\n\nEach line has a different slope, but both reach the x-axis at $x=1$. That shared point is the intersection. Check it in both original equations: $3(1)+0=3$ and $1+2(0)=1$. Include the graph as requested; listing the point without drawing the lines leaves the graphical task unfinished."}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "94273f38-45f8-5317-8c4a-f76b39a4c502"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405141} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "94273f38-45f8-5317-8c4a-f76b39a4c502" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050796}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405515}] [[:question/id #uuid "2303b6fa-644e-57a3-9106-b23121ca0f8e"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405147 :question/id #uuid "2303b6fa-644e-57a3-9106-b23121ca0f8e" :question/problem "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nExplain the geometric significance of: No solution." :question/worked-solution "There is no point common to all $k$ lines. Their common intersection is empty.\n\nA solution must satisfy every equation at once. Two distinct parallel lines already prevent a solution. With three or more lines, each pair can intersect at a different point while no point lies on all the lines; pairwise intersection alone is insufficient."}] [[:question/id #uuid "252e7f01-4105-5dd3-aafb-4821a8db1687"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405156 :question/id #uuid "252e7f01-4105-5dd3-aafb-4821a8db1687" :question/problem "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nExplain the geometric significance of: A unique solution." :question/worked-solution "All $k$ lines have exactly one common point. The coordinates of that point form the unique solution.\n\nFind a point on two distinct intersecting lines, then check that every remaining line also passes through it. Coincident copies of one line do not create additional solutions once another distinct line fixes a single intersection."}] [[:question/id #uuid "61b02812-4719-5d6e-aaba-a96faba07692"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405345 :question/id #uuid "61b02812-4719-5d6e-aaba-a96faba07692" :question/problem "**Question 3 — Part C**\n\nExplain the geometric significance of: An infinite number of solutions." :question/worked-solution "All $k$ lines coincide: they describe the same geometric line. Every point on that line satisfies every equation, so there are infinitely many solutions.\n\nTwo distinct lines can share at most one point. Therefore infinitely many common points require every equation to describe the same line. Distinct parallel lines have no common points, so being parallel is not enough."}] [[:step/id #uuid "59ec431f-6761-599b-ab37-cdbc022279fe"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405147} :step/id #uuid "59ec431f-6761-599b-ab37-cdbc022279fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405738}}] [[:step/id #uuid "dfa44c60-0cb7-5441-abdb-da153f2b5342"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405156} :step/id #uuid "dfa44c60-0cb7-5441-abdb-da153f2b5342" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405557}}] [[:step/id #uuid "a2befd69-b641-58ff-a358-ba83948714b1"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405345} :step/id #uuid "a2befd69-b641-58ff-a358-ba83948714b1"}] [[:multistep/id #uuid "3d0c16a2-0d5a-55ba-a538-fa1e58cb9b70"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405233 :multistep/context "Source exercise: **1.1.3**, [printed page 7 (PDF page 19)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=19).\n\nYou have a system of $k$ equations in two variables, $k\\geq 2$. Explain the geometric significance of:" :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405323} :multistep/id #uuid "3d0c16a2-0d5a-55ba-a538-fa1e58cb9b70" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405147} :step/id #uuid "59ec431f-6761-599b-ab37-cdbc022279fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405345} :step/id #uuid "a2befd69-b641-58ff-a358-ba83948714b1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405156} :step/id #uuid "dfa44c60-0cb7-5441-abdb-da153f2b5342" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405557}}]}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "04e594fb-c67a-5d9d-a4ba-54d4721adffb"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405233} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "04e594fb-c67a-5d9d-a4ba-54d4721adffb" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050246}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405066}] [[:answer/id #uuid "1a50646c-61b5-542c-ae9a-c93c95409a53"] {:answer/id #uuid "1a50646c-61b5-542c-ae9a-c93c95409a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(10/13,1/13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405882}] [[:answer-field/id #uuid "bb04ac26-d540-5d4c-8990-9e541f76949e"] {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a50646c-61b5-542c-ae9a-c93c95409a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(10/13,1/13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405882} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb04ac26-d540-5d4c-8990-9e541f76949e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406193}] [[:question/id #uuid "7108b45a-bee1-5b0d-a9fa-58c7694ce7cd"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a50646c-61b5-542c-ae9a-c93c95409a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(10/13,1/13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405882} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb04ac26-d540-5d4c-8990-9e541f76949e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406193}] :question/id #uuid "7108b45a-bee1-5b0d-a9fa-58c7694ce7cd" :question/problem "Source exercise: **1.2.1**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\n**Question 4**\n\nFind the point $(x_1,y_1)$ which lies on both lines,\n\n$$\nx+3y=1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4x-y=3.\n$$\n\nEnter the exact ordered pair $(x_1,y_1)$, with fractions simplified: {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A common point satisfies both equations. Multiply $x+3y=1$ by $4$ to get $4x+12y=4$, then subtract $4x-y=3$. This gives $13y=1$, so $y=1/13$ and $x=1-3/13=10/13$. Thus the point is $\\left(\\frac{10}{13},\\frac{1}{13}\\right)$. Substitution gives $1$ and $3$ in the two original equations. Enter $x$ first and $y$ second."}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "bb299914-611a-54e8-850f-f40bb23dda37"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405404} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "bb299914-611a-54e8-850f-f40bb23dda37" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050196}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405632}] [[:answer/id #uuid "23c82332-4a8d-5804-895d-493d128e52bc"] {:answer/id #uuid "23c82332-4a8d-5804-895d-493d128e52bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405902}] [[:answer-field/id #uuid "fe6200bd-309b-51b5-980b-b805488ffef3"] {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c82332-4a8d-5804-895d-493d128e52bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405902}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405902} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6200bd-309b-51b5-980b-b805488ffef3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406332}] [[:question/id #uuid "a7d59dcf-2026-53bb-9f01-204b0bb6cd64"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c82332-4a8d-5804-895d-493d128e52bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405902}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405902} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6200bd-309b-51b5-980b-b805488ffef3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406332}] :question/id #uuid "a7d59dcf-2026-53bb-9f01-204b0bb6cd64" :question/problem "Source exercise: **1.2.2**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\n**Question 5**\n\nFind the point of intersection of the two lines\n\n$$\n3x+y=3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x+2y=1.\n$$\n\nEnter the exact ordered pair $(x,y)$: {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Eliminate $y$ to find the point satisfying both equations. Twice the first equation is $6x+2y=6$; subtracting $x+2y=1$ gives $5x=5$. Hence $x=1$, and $y=3-3x=0$. The point $(1,0)$ satisfies $3x+y=3$ and $x+2y=1$. A zero coordinate is valid: it means this intersection lies on the x-axis."}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "6fceb2a5-80b2-51fa-8637-69c79216f727"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405568} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "6fceb2a5-80b2-51fa-8637-69c79216f727" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050196}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405397}] [[:question/id #uuid "9a3ab431-ea46-5c56-b51b-2479efd1cb3f"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405531 :question/id #uuid "9a3ab431-ea46-5c56-b51b-2479efd1cb3f" :question/problem "Source exercise: **1.2.3**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\n**Question 6**\n\nDo the three lines\n\n$$\nx+2y=1, \\qquad 2x-y=1, \\qquad 4x+3y=3\n$$\n\nhave a common point of intersection? If so, find the point; if not, tell why they do not have such a common point of intersection." :question/worked-solution "Yes. All three lines meet at $\\left(\\frac35,\\frac15\\right)$.\n\nFrom $x+2y=1$, write $x=1-2y$. Substitute into $2x-y=1$:\n\n$$2(1-2y)-y=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 2-5y=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac15.$$\n\nThen $x=1-2/5=3/5$. Check the remaining equation:\n\n$$4\\left(\\frac35\\right)+3\\left(\\frac15\\right)=\\frac{12}{5}+\\frac35=3.$$\n\nThe point obtained from the first two lines lies on the third, so it is common to all three.\n\nSolving two equations gives a candidate, not yet a solution to a three-equation system. Always substitute the candidate into the remaining equation. Here it satisfies the third equation as well. The first two lines are distinct and have only one intersection, so the verified common point is unique."}] [[:assigned-problem/id #uuid "df38b813-7b1f-58a1-b938-d0468354bf9e"] {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405531} :assigned-problem/id #uuid "df38b813-7b1f-58a1-b938-d0468354bf9e" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048832}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405736}] [[:step/id #uuid "79eec771-0417-53b5-a386-6e08f10f94c5"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405239} :step/id #uuid "79eec771-0417-53b5-a386-6e08f10f94c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405275}}] [[:step/id #uuid "47fc78a6-53cb-5dc4-a296-8373dbc8c6c1"] {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405510} :step/id #uuid "47fc78a6-53cb-5dc4-a296