7592186405298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405144} :step/id #uuid "413869a6-4e38-5e62-82a1-a5d93522667e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405646} :step/id #uuid "47f845ae-462c-5d63-929d-d246cffaf3f1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405641} :step/id #uuid "4f2a3008-2156-556f-9ba1-d0ce4b2b1e7c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405351}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405523} :step/id #uuid "63ce76ac-4de7-580c-a2f5-398f2420a1e7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405164} :step/id #uuid "8ffc03a7-3cdf-53c0-a513-2906efa6f95e"}] :activity/title "MTH 255 — WHW-2" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045461} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405412}] [[:course/id #uuid "b2e5af5c-39d0-53ab-8fc6-a2a70c44d25e"] {:course/code "MTH 251" :course/description "Differential Calculus introduces the mathematics of change for real-valued functions of one variable. Building on algebra and trigonometry, the course develops the concepts and methods needed to interpret derivatives, analyze functions, and solve problems involving changing quantities. Graphical, numerical, and symbolic representations connect computational techniques with mathematical reasoning and applications." :course/id #uuid "b2e5af5c-39d0-53ab-8fc6-a2a70c44d25e" :course/outcomes [{:course-outcome/category "Function Analysis and Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "219db5a6-b013-55dc-84f6-a6af421dbaa2" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Formulate and solve maximum and minimum problems using differential calculus." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406336} {:course-outcome/category "Function Analysis and Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "6b02704b-77f0-59f1-b300-7d1ad5522589" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Use derivatives to analyze motion, including relationships among position, velocity, speed, and acceleration." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406337} {:course-outcome/category "Function Analysis and Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "794f739d-0e50-5e45-952a-ca590ac4b91e" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Determine the shape and behavior of a graph using derivative information, including increasing and decreasing behavior, extrema, and concavity." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406338} {:course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" :course-outcome/id #uuid "8e0b28cf-8e52-5504-95a8-4e9b239c85a5" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Differentiate single-variable functions using standard differentiation rules, including the chain rule." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406339} {:course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" :course-outcome/id #uuid "e11ac0b7-168a-53f3-9ca4-4dc90089617e" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Evaluate limits using algebraic limit laws and interpret limiting behavior through graphical, numerical, and symbolic representations." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406340} {:course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" :course-outcome/id #uuid "e859a4bd-288c-5aef-9572-3dcf309d9852" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Calculate average and instantaneous rates of change and explain how the derivative represents an instantaneous rate." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406341} {:course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" :course-outcome/id #uuid "f6479bab-c0ae-5920-bba4-64293ec407a7" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Analyze the continuity of a single-variable function at a point or over an interval." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406342} {:course-outcome/category "Mathematical Modeling and Reasoning" :course-outcome/id #uuid "0492ff25-34f8-58e0-8deb-997fcf89ad94" :course-outcome/index 9 :course-outcome/text "Evaluate the suitability of a mathematical approach and the validity of its conclusions, accounting for the assumptions used in constructing the model." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406343} {:course-outcome/category "Mathematical Modeling and Reasoning" :course-outcome/id #uuid "5d9d7b2f-fee2-5bef-9ba6-2ce5add42a5d" :course-outcome/index 8 :course-outcome/text "Recognize situations that can be modeled mathematically, identify relevant quantities, and calculate or estimate the relationships needed to solve a problem." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406344}] :course/overview "Limits and continuity provide the conceptual framework for studying derivatives. Students connect average rates of change with instantaneous rates, then use limit laws and differentiation rules—including the chain rule—to calculate and interpret derivatives. The emphasis is on understanding what these calculations represent, rather than treating differentiation solely as algebraic manipulation.\n\nApplications connect derivatives to motion, the shape of a graph, and maximum and minimum problems. Students use calculus to analyze velocity, speed, and acceleration; investigate how functions change; and solve optimization problems. Mathematical modeling includes identifying relevant variables, making appropriate assumptions, and evaluating whether a method and its conclusions make sense in context." :course/school "Oregon State University" :course/title "Differential Calculus" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405040}] [[:course/id #uuid "72faeb91-1b34-5252-b9a7-456659fc2b8c"] {:course/code "MTH 252" :course/description "Integral Calculus studies accumulation and integration for functions of one variable. Building on differential calculus, the course develops definite and indefinite integrals and their applications. Graphical, numerical, and symbolic approaches support the interpretation of integrals and their use in mathematical modeling and problem solving." :course/id #uuid "72faeb91-1b34-5252-b9a7-456659fc2b8c" :course/outcomes [{:course-outcome/category "Foundations of Integration" :course-outcome/id #uuid "9d9f2f48-039f-5217-b7b2-a6b64a7b94c2" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Find antiderivatives and distinguish indefinite integrals from definite integrals." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406389} {:course-outcome/category "Foundations of Integration" :course-outcome/id #uuid "e1fe3e3d-fdc9-57c6-82fe-78d2079e4886" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Interpret Riemann sums and definite integrals graphically and as signed accumulation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406390} {:course-outcome/category "Foundations of Integration" :course-outcome/id #uuid "0f810e7c-6d63-5dc7-a416-6c8e6b1e0913" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Apply the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate definite integrals and differentiate integrals with variable limits." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406391} {:course-outcome/category "Geometric and Physical Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "3b8afaeb-63e0-509b-8cdd-cc346c364684" :course-outcome/index 8 :course-outcome/text "Use integration to calculate curve lengths and physical quantities, including displacement, total distance, force, and work." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406392} {:course-outcome/category "Geometric and Physical Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "3baf45c7-8ce5-5b29-804e-716fa9a6b746" :course-outcome/index 9 :course-outcome/text "Formulate and solve elementary first-order differential equations describing growth and decay." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406393} {:course-outcome/category "Geometric and Physical Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "3bf81423-f351-5d90-80ab-2906960358da" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Calculate areas, including areas described in polar coordinates, and volumes using slicing or cylindrical shells." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406394} {:course-outcome/category "Integration Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "94786ab2-4a1a-5c1e-b76f-4f4f7bf220ca" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Evaluate improper integrals using limits and determine whether they converge." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406395} {:course-outcome/category "Integration Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "9e16c6c4-7123-5514-bfa3-5e97b7f45018" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Select and apply substitution, integration by parts, trigonometric integration methods, and partial fractions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406396} {:course-outcome/category "Integration Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "a998873a-721a-57ac-b84c-6a7a8dcef7be" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Approximate definite integrals using basic numerical integration rules." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406397}] :course/overview "The course connects Riemann sums, definite integrals, and antiderivatives through the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. Students develop integration techniques and use numerical approximation alongside analytical methods. These tools support applications to areas, volumes, curve lengths, motion, force, and work. Logarithmic and exponential functions, polar coordinates, improper integrals, and elementary growth and decay models extend the range of problems addressed." :course/school "Oregon State University" :course/title "Integral Calculus" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405041}] [[:course/id #uuid "e9dc19ff-a53b-51f7-bd17-d1b14351322a"] {:course/code "MTH 254" :course/description "Vector Calculus I extends differentiation and integration to vector-valued functions and functions of several variables. Building on single-variable calculus, the course develops mathematical tools for describing curves, surfaces, motion, and quantities distributed across regions in two or three dimensions. Geometric interpretation and physical applications connect vector operations, multivariable derivatives, and multiple integrals." :course/id #uuid "e9dc19ff-a53b-51f7-bd17-d1b14351322a" :course/outcomes [{:course-outcome/category "Multiple Integration" :course-outcome/id #uuid "48a43247-d19d-5bcb-b5ca-c75ddbf4080d" :course-outcome/index 11 :course-outcome/text "Set up and evaluate double and triple integrals over specified regions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406355} {:course-outcome/category "Multiple Integration" :course-outcome/id #uuid "50dd9302-e5bf-5bd3-90be-5e44b6db61fa" :course-outcome/index 12 :course-outcome/text "Select suitable coordinates and apply changes of variables, including the appropriate area or volume scaling factor." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406356} {:course-outcome/category "Multiple Integration" :course-outcome/id #uuid "7c28ceff-35c9-5d29-a46e-92392c3024c6" :course-outcome/index 13 :course-outcome/text "Use multiple integrals to calculate geometric and physical quantities, including volume and mass." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406357} {:course-outcome/category "Multivariable Differentiation and Optimization" :course-outcome/id #uuid "9a78a88e-3c1e-5179-a3a0-5bb4ef9e8a5b" :course-outcome/index 9 :course-outcome/text "Construct tangent planes and use linear approximations to estimate nearby function values." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406358} {:course-outcome/category "Multivariable Differentiation and Optimization" :course-outcome/id #uuid "9c17afa0-6af5-5717-9945-124a8e2c5fe9" :course-outcome/index 10 :course-outcome/text "Find and classify critical points and solve unconstrained and constrained optimization problems, including problems requiring Lagrange multipliers." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406359} {:course-outcome/category "Multivariable Differentiation and Optimization" :course-outcome/id #uuid "d0c55d89-b69c-5141-ab2a-14bcfa56b84a" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Calculate partial derivatives and apply the multivariable chain rule." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406360} {:course-outcome/category "Multivariable Differentiation and Optimization" :course-outcome/id #uuid "2c4ed1eb-3abb-55f1-b187-73c15346e09b" :course-outcome/index 8 :course-outcome/text "Use gradients and directional derivatives to determine rates and directions of change and relate them to level sets." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406361} {:course-outcome/category "Multivariable Differentiation and Optimization" :course-outcome/id #uuid "47d38675-dd90-5ae2-902d-f0824b76ad82" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Evaluate limits and analyze continuity for functions of several variables." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406362} {:course-outcome/category "Vector-Valued Functions and Curves" :course-outcome/id #uuid "d027fd3a-14c1-57ad-b934-a1539ea5111c" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Calculate arc length and curvature and interpret tangent and normal vectors as descriptions of a curve’s local geometry." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406363} {:course-outcome/category "Vector-Valued Functions and Curves" :course-outcome/id #uuid "3380e0d8-0f1a-5f51-9026-c5faef1771a8" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Differentiate and integrate vector-valued functions and use them to describe position, velocity, and acceleration." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406364} {:course-outcome/category "Vectors, Coordinates, and Spatial Geometry" :course-outcome/id #uuid "d8b2769d-cef4-5b6e-aaec-ef6eff7468e9" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Represent and analyze lines, planes, surfaces, and level sets using equations and geometric descriptions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406365} {:course-outcome/category "Vectors, Coordinates, and Spatial Geometry" :course-outcome/id #uuid "1f022312-9abe-5b04-a5f8-7ef76765a398" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Describe points and regions using rectangular, polar, cylindrical, and spherical coordinates and convert between appropriate coordinate systems." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406366} {:course-outcome/category "Vectors, Coordinates, and Spatial Geometry" :course-outcome/id #uuid "65d6478b-61f1-5af6-9344-7d002c435d42" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Perform vector operations, including dot and cross products, and interpret their geometric meaning." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406367}] :course/overview "Vectors and coordinate systems provide a language for describing geometry in the plane and in space. Vector-valued functions represent curves and motion, while derivatives and integrals of these functions describe velocity, acceleration, arc length, and curvature. Equations of surfaces and their level sets extend graphical reasoning to functions of several variables.\n\nMultivariable differentiation includes limits, continuity, partial derivatives, the chain rule, directional derivatives, and gradients. These concepts support tangent-plane approximations and optimization, including constrained problems using Lagrange multipliers. Multiple integration addresses accumulated quantities over planar and spatial regions. Rectangular, polar, cylindrical, and spherical coordinates—and more general changes of variables—allow students to match an integral’s representation to the geometry of its region." :course/school "Oregon State University" :course/title "Vector Calculus I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405042}] [[:course/id #uuid "c37c596e-77c4-5554-8295-443fb5da076b"] {:course/code "MTH 255" :course/description "Vector Calculus II develops the calculus of vector fields and integration along curves and over surfaces. Building on Vector Calculus I, the course connects local descriptions of a field with accumulated quantities such as work, circulation, and flux. Its central tools include conservative fields, divergence, curl, and the integral theorems of Gauss and Stokes, with applications involving force, work, mass, and charge." :course/id #uuid "c37c596e-77c4-5554-8295-443fb5da076b" :course/outcomes [{:course-outcome/category "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems" :course-outcome/id #uuid "8b26130c-3ade-59f1-b5cd-30c4e77a4ee2" :course-outcome/index 9 :course-outcome/text "Explain and apply the Divergence Theorem to connect flux through a closed surface with a volume integral." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406345} {:course-outcome/category "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems" :course-outcome/id #uuid "11744f40-4941-5b05-bf74-21cff6dfbc86" :course-outcome/index 10 :course-outcome/text "Explain and apply Stokes’ Theorem to connect circulation around a boundary curve with a surface integral of curl." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406346} {:course-outcome/category "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems" :course-outcome/id #uuid "68ca80d7-e11e-58c2-9696-309503e57498" :course-outcome/index 8 :course-outcome/text "Calculate divergence and curl in standard coordinate systems and interpret them in relation to flux and circulation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406347} {:course-outcome/category "Line Integrals and Conservative Fields" :course-outcome/id #uuid "9cd133e0-c54b-57f1-b597-4c2164a5bb07" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Analyze conservative fields, find potential functions when they exist, and use path independence to evaluate line integrals." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406348} {:course-outcome/category "Line Integrals and Conservative Fields" :course-outcome/id #uuid "da10e544-4158-5cc3-9189-961bf8e19bf4" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Evaluate scalar and vector line integrals and interpret their use in calculating quantities such as mass, work, and circulation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406349} {:course-outcome/category "Surface Integrals and Flux" :course-outcome/id #uuid "c94983bd-7208-5af7-ab33-1ac7d94075a5" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Set up and evaluate scalar surface integrals for quantities distributed over a surface, including mass or charge." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406350} {:course-outcome/category "Surface Integrals and Flux" :course-outcome/id #uuid "25423c4b-02b3-5fec-9546-7129ca1e1f95" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Calculate flux through an oriented surface and interpret the role of the field’s normal component." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406351} {:course-outcome/category "Vector Geometry and Field Interpretation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "b93f8b7e-2e08-5fbc-b3b3-5fd74c01da7c" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Explain the relationship among gradients, directional derivatives, and level sets." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406352} {:course-outcome/category "Vector Geometry and Field Interpretation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "3561ac1e-b8dd-59c8-b44a-28a8ad8f9fdc" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Express vectors in standard coordinate systems and bases and interpret dot and cross products geometrically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406353} {:course-outcome/category "Vector Geometry and Field Interpretation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "6ad6c8c5-38f2-5456-9c16-b54686cf0083" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Construct line and surface elements appropriate to a curve or surface, accounting for its geometry and orientation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406354}] :course/overview "The course revisits vector functions, space curves, gradients, and directional derivatives before extending integration to curves and surfaces. Line integrals connect vector fields with work and circulation. Conservative fields, potential functions, and path independence explain when an integral can be determined from endpoint information rather than the details of a path.\n\nSurface geometry and oriented surface elements provide the foundation for surface integrals and flux. Divergence and curl describe local field behavior, while the Divergence Theorem and Stokes’ Theorem connect these local quantities to integrals over regions and their boundaries. The archived course materials emphasize geometric understanding and the use of vector differentials to connect the various integration methods." :course/school "Oregon State University" :course/title "Vector Calculus II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405043}] [[:course/id #uuid "fa274c9b-ca65-5fde-b8ed-49681698e235"] {:course/code "MTH 256" :course/description "Applied Differential Equations introduces methods for describing and solving problems in which an unknown function is related to its derivatives. The course focuses on ordinary differential equations and applications appropriate to science and engineering, particularly electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators. Coverage includes first-order linear and selected nonlinear equations, second-order linear equations, and introductory work with higher-order equations and Laplace transforms." :course/id #uuid "fa274c9b-ca65-5fde-b8ed-49681698e235" :course/outcomes [{:course-outcome/category "Analytical Solution Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "b27b909d-406b-5016-9dba-6d6cde8fe738" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Apply introductory solution methods to higher-order linear differential equations." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406368} {:course-outcome/category "Analytical Solution Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "f3d63c4b-0f30-5288-8faf-fd7effcd7d55" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Obtain solutions of second-order linear differential equations." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406369} {:course-outcome/category "Analytical Solution Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "5879a21c-ee5c-5351-9547-766af3a0a0b4" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Solve first-order linear equations and selected first-order nonlinear equations using methods appropriate to their form." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406370} {:course-outcome/category "Electrical and Mechanical Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "7677d590-eded-5f0e-a320-fd5a22fef284" :course-outcome/index 9 :course-outcome/text "Formulate and solve differential-equation problems involving electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators, interpreting the resulting functions in terms of the modeled system." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406371} {:course-outcome/category "Equation Classification and Mathematical Modeling" :course-outcome/id #uuid "a8623707-6d3b-55ce-b413-a7dcb5686dd4" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Interpret the variables, derivatives, and solutions of a differential-equation model in the context of the problem." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406372} {:course-outcome/category "Equation Classification and Mathematical Modeling" :course-outcome/id #uuid "b3f87601-f5f0-5265-a60a-d5faa7f8ef26" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Classify ordinary differential equations by order and distinguish linear equations from nonlinear equations." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406373} {:course-outcome/category "Equation Classification and Mathematical Modeling" :course-outcome/id #uuid "06f1cd43-7150-5138-9257-b06e58024ccf" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Translate a basic scientific or engineering model into a differential equation relating an unknown function to its rates of change." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406374} {:course-outcome/category "Laplace Transform Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "d14ff5cc-c727-5ac4-80dc-396644cb4d2d" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Explain how a Laplace transform can convert a linear differential-equation problem into an algebraic problem." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406375} {:course-outcome/category "Laplace Transform Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "1a7956a5-b684-551c-83df-ccea47ac1ba4" :course-outcome/index 8 :course-outcome/text "Use elementary Laplace transforms and inverse transforms to solve basic linear differential-equation problems." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406376}] :course/overview "The archived syllabus begins with mathematical modeling and the classification of differential equations, then develops methods for first-order and second-order equations. This organization connects the structure of an equation with an appropriate solution approach. Applications give meaning to the unknown functions and their derivatives rather than treating equations as purely formal expressions.\n\nLaplace transforms provide an additional approach to solving differential equations, while introductory higher-order work extends the range of equations considered. Electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators serve as central application settings. The published description also identifies familiarity with complex numbers and Euler’s identities as relevant background." :course/school "Oregon State University" :course/title "Applied Differential Equations" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405044}] [[:course/id #uuid "3ace2150-672f-50e7-9c2c-027b6d4272d7"] {:course/code "MTH 341" :course/description "Linear Algebra I introduces the computational and conceptual study of linear systems, matrices, and subspaces. The course connects methods for solving equations with the structure of linear transformations and vector spaces. Determinants, linear independence, bases, dimension, and introductory eigenvalue analysis provide complementary ways to understand how a linear system or transformation behaves." :course/id #uuid "3ace2150-672f-50e7-9c2c-027b6d4272d7" :course/outcomes [{:course-outcome/category "Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Introductory Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "c21e5c95-26cd-5756-9092-0f79c13dc069" :course-outcome/index 12 :course-outcome/text "Explain, at an introductory level, how eigenvalue methods can be used in solving simple systems of linear differential equations." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406377} {:course-outcome/category "Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Introductory Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "d4780100-cd7f-58a2-8145-face3fff2460" :course-outcome/index 11 :course-outcome/text "Relate algebraic and geometric multiplicities to the availability of an eigenvector basis and diagonalize a matrix when such a basis exists." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406378} {:course-outcome/category "Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Introductory Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "6b02fb05-51ae-5983-9ed2-7e2763532ea8" :course-outcome/index 10 :course-outcome/text "Calculate eigenvalues and eigenvectors, including examples involving nonreal complex values, and determine the associated eigenspaces." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406379} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "15723e90-f0cc-5ee0-9f2e-05c5f5aed323" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Use Gaussian elimination to solve linear systems and describe their complete solution sets." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406380} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "26f3092c-2231-5b66-80de-d4a832531472" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Calculate determinants and use their properties to assess invertibility and the uniqueness of solutions to square linear systems." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406381} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "3ef6ac4e-6cad-594f-83a6-5f66d9769392" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Perform matrix operations and determine whether a square matrix has an inverse, calculating it when one exists." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406382} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Transformations" :course-outcome/id #uuid "a152871e-0fe3-5370-8e14-1c769972f5c4" :course-outcome/index 9 :course-outcome/text "Interpret matrix transformations geometrically and relate composition of transformations to matrix multiplication." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406383} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Transformations" :course-outcome/id #uuid "0ab3c082-09e3-558c-8ee4-d04898b4949c" :course-outcome/index 8 :course-outcome/text "Construct and use the standard-basis matrix representation of a linear transformation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406384} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "8c76e2f7-3c8c-5569-b169-2cc27e8fa04d" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Apply the rank–nullity relationship to reason about solution sets and matrix invertibility." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406385} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "d28777c9-3775-500a-8e78-8f1ec68fe02a" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Analyze linear dependence and independence and determine the span of a collection of vectors." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406386} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "35e7d779-26db-56d0-9c90-8d096e4d6754" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Find bases and dimensions for row, column, and null spaces." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406387} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "753ae7d6-d7b2-545e-b987-f70e367561c7" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Determine whether a subset of \\(\\mathbb{R}^n\\) satisfies the requirements for a subspace." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406388}] :course/overview "Work with linear systems and Gaussian elimination establishes a foundation for matrix operations, invertibility, and determinants. The course then connects these calculations with linear independence, spanning sets, subspaces, bases, and dimension. Row, column, and null spaces describe important features of a matrix, while rank–nullity relates those spaces to the structure of a system’s solutions.\n\nMatrix representations make linear transformations concrete through examples such as rotation, reflection, projection, and scaling. Eigenvalues and eigenvectors introduce directions preserved by a transformation, including examples with complex eigenvalues. The archived section used here also introduces diagonalization and illustrates eigenvalue methods through simple systems of differential equations." :course/school "Oregon State University" :course/title "Linear Algebra I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405045}] [[:course-group/id #uuid "3bf14d1a-f9f9-51e5-9404-d0b3fa5f729b"] {:course-group/courses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405045}] :course-group/id #uuid "3bf14d1a-f9f9-51e5-9404-d0b3fa5f729b" :course-group/title "OSU-MTH" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405039}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "e11ac0b7-168a-53f3-9ca4-4dc90089617e"] {:course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" :course-outcome/id #uuid "e11ac0b7-168a-53f3-9ca4-4dc90089617e" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Evaluate limits using algebraic limit laws and interpret limiting behavior through graphical, numerical, and symbolic representations." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406340}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "f6479bab-c0ae-5920-bba4-64293ec407a7"] {:course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" :course-outcome/id #uuid "f6479bab-c0ae-5920-bba4-64293ec407a7" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Analyze the continuity of a single-variable function at a point or over an interval." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406342}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "e859a4bd-288c-5aef-9572-3dcf309d9852"] {:course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" :course-outcome/id #uuid "e859a4bd-288c-5aef-9572-3dcf309d9852" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Calculate average and instantaneous rates of change and explain how the derivative represents an instantaneous rate." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406341}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "8e0b28cf-8e52-5504-95a8-4e9b239c85a5"] {:course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" :course-outcome/id #uuid "8e0b28cf-8e52-5504-95a8-4e9b239c85a5" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Differentiate single-variable functions using standard differentiation rules, including the chain rule." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406339}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "6b02704b-77f0-59f1-b300-7d1ad5522589"] {:course-outcome/category "Function Analysis and Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "6b02704b-77f0-59f1-b300-7d1ad5522589" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Use derivatives to analyze motion, including relationships among position, velocity, speed, and acceleration." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406337}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "794f739d-0e50-5e45-952a-ca590ac4b91e"] {:course-outcome/category "Function Analysis and Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "794f739d-0e50-5e45-952a-ca590ac4b91e" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Determine the shape and behavior of a graph using derivative information, including increasing and decreasing behavior, extrema, and concavity." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406338}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "219db5a6-b013-55dc-84f6-a6af421dbaa2"] {:course-outcome/category "Function Analysis and Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "219db5a6-b013-55dc-84f6-a6af421dbaa2" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Formulate and solve maximum and minimum problems using differential calculus." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406336}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "5d9d7b2f-fee2-5bef-9ba6-2ce5add42a5d"] {:course-outcome/category "Mathematical Modeling and Reasoning" :course-outcome/id #uuid "5d9d7b2f-fee2-5bef-9ba6-2ce5add42a5d" :course-outcome/index 8 :course-outcome/text "Recognize situations that can be modeled mathematically, identify relevant quantities, and calculate or estimate the relationships needed to solve a problem." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406344}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "0492ff25-34f8-58e0-8deb-997fcf89ad94"] {:course-outcome/category "Mathematical Modeling and Reasoning" :course-outcome/id #uuid "0492ff25-34f8-58e0-8deb-997fcf89ad94" :course-outcome/index 9 :course-outcome/text "Evaluate the suitability of a mathematical approach and the validity of its conclusions, accounting for the assumptions used in constructing the model." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406343}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "e1fe3e3d-fdc9-57c6-82fe-78d2079e4886"] {:course-outcome/category "Foundations of Integration" :course-outcome/id #uuid "e1fe3e3d-fdc9-57c6-82fe-78d2079e4886" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Interpret Riemann sums and definite integrals graphically and as signed accumulation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406390}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "9d9f2f48-039f-5217-b7b2-a6b64a7b94c2"] {:course-outcome/category "Foundations of Integration" :course-outcome/id #uuid "9d9f2f48-039f-5217-b7b2-a6b64a7b94c2" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Find antiderivatives and distinguish indefinite integrals from definite integrals." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406389}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "0f810e7c-6d63-5dc7-a416-6c8e6b1e0913"] {:course-outcome/category "Foundations of Integration" :course-outcome/id #uuid "0f810e7c-6d63-5dc7-a416-6c8e6b1e0913" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Apply the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate definite integrals and differentiate integrals with variable limits." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406391}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "9e16c6c4-7123-5514-bfa3-5e97b7f45018"] {:course-outcome/category "Integration Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "9e16c6c4-7123-5514-bfa3-5e97b7f45018" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Select and apply substitution, integration by parts, trigonometric integration methods, and partial fractions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406396}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "a998873a-721a-57ac-b84c-6a7a8dcef7be"] {:course-outcome/category "Integration Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "a998873a-721a-57ac-b84c-6a7a8dcef7be" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Approximate definite integrals using basic numerical integration rules." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406397}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "94786ab2-4a1a-5c1e-b76f-4f4f7bf220ca"] {:course-outcome/category "Integration Methods" :course-outcome/id #uuid "94786ab2-4a1a-5c1e-b76f-4f4f7bf220ca" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Evaluate improper integrals using limits and determine whether they converge." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406395}] [[:course-outcome/id #uuid "3bf81423-f351-5d90-80ab-2906960358da"] {:course-outcome/category "Geometric and Physical Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "3bf81423-f351-5d90-80ab-2906960358da" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Calculate areas, including areas described in polar coordinates, and volumes using slicing or cylindrical shells." :db/i